{"title":"Mathematical problem solving","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The idea behind The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Art_of_Problem_Solving\" title=\"Art of Problem Solving\">Art of Problem Solving</a> as well as many <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Math_competitions\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Math competitions\">math competitions</a> is the use of creative methods to solve problems. In a way, students are discouraged to use rote memorization as opposed to creative spontaneous thinking. Mathematical <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Problem_solving\" title=\"Problem solving\">problem solving</a> involves using all the tools at one's disposal to attack a problem in a new way.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"A_Historical_Example\">A Historical Example</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mathematical_problem_solving&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: A Historical Example\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>An interesting example of this kind of thinking is the calculation of the sum of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Series\" title=\"Series\">series</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/1/5c1597ebecfe2622e7a20a85f2a92705c9510786.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac11 + \\frac14 + \\frac19 + \\cdots + \\frac{1}{n^2} + \\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"213\" height=\"37\" /><br />\nThe famous <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mathematician\" title=\"Mathematician\">mathematician</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Leonhard_Euler\" title=\"Leonhard Euler\">Leonhard Euler</a> used the fact that:<br />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/3/c63b2c013e47963b4b8c0c45d141408bef94bd73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin{x}=x-\\frac{x^3}{3!}+\\frac{x^5}{5!}-\\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"200\" height=\"39\" /><br />\n</p><p>The zeros of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/1/f41470508df2ceb87f97c7e939589024bceb16e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin{x}$\" width=\"35\" height=\"12\" /> are at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/8/cf81fc37254c09a2126d81baf38c9922dd2fdf41.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pm \\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/2/f22da63125f2b769d114be711a42a364fb1ceecf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pm{2\\pi}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" />, etc. so Euler made the leap of claiming that the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial\" title=\"Polynomial\">polynomial</a> on the right hand side can be factored as<br />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/8/5a8124e336a740db7eaa1946dc7f973bcd0fe00f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x-\\frac{x^3}{3!}+\\frac{x^5}{5!}-\\cdots=x\\left(1-\\frac{x}{\\pi}\\right)\\left(1+\\frac{x}{\\pi}\\right)\\left(1-\\frac{x}{2\\pi}\\right)\\left(1+\\frac{x}{2\\pi}\\right)\\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"499\" height=\"39\" /><br />\n</p><p>since both sides are 0 at the same places. Dividing both sides by x and simplifying the right side, we get<br />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/b/e0b5f7ed014cce1ff98e2987d6ac53a8116ed51a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-\\frac{x^2}{3!}+\\frac{x^4}{5!}-\\cdots=\\left(1-\\frac{x^2}{\\pi^2}\\right)\\left(1-\\frac{x^2}{4\\pi^2}\\right)\\left(1-\\frac{x^2}{9\\pi^2}\\right)\\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"451\" height=\"43\" /><br />\n</p><p>The constant terms of both sides agree, both being 1, so this crazy procedure might be valid. Setting the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76b2878564ab19b80abb21ba964abe90fe120846.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2$\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" /> coefficients equal, we have<br />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/e/9fef4e29638739fa42324849a14f7fe33d02fe7a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-\\frac16 = -\\frac{1}{\\pi^2}-\\frac{1}{4\\pi^2}-\\frac{1}{9\\pi^2}-\\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"237\" height=\"37\" /><br />\n</p><p>or, multiplying both sides by -<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/4/0a490286316c84d1b6016469f9e03bee57ed7d67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi^2$\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" />,<br />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/5/f457fd1212e17cc87528f4f8ffcf8826ce7b67e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\pi^2}{6}=\\frac{1}{1^2}+\\frac{1}{2^2}+\\frac{1}{3^2}+\\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"194\" height=\"39\" /><br />\n</p><p><i><b>-Quoted from <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Store/viewitem.php?item=ps:aops2\">Art of Problem Solving Volume 2</a>, page 258</b></i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018074758\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.011 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.022 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 42/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 2540/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:1648:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018074758 and revision id 61863. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}